(* ==========================================================================
   Buri — Extended BNF grammar
   Version 0.3 (draft)
   File extension: .buri

   NOTATION
     A ::= B          definition
     A | B            alternation
     ( ... )          grouping
     X?               zero or one
     X*               zero or more
     X+               one or more
     "..."            terminal (literal token text)
     "a".."z"         a range of characters (lexical grammar only)
     UPPER_CAMEL      non-terminal
     ALL_CAPS         lexical token class (see LEXICAL GRAMMAR at the end)
     name=X           a label: what a syntax tree calls its capture of X
     (* ... *)        comment
     (* @... *)       a directive (see GENERATING A PARSER, below)

   PROPERTIES
     The syntactic grammar below is context-free and UNAMBIGUOUS: no string
     of tokens has two parse trees. No production requires knowledge of what a
     name refers to; no production requires "feedback" from the type checker
     or from name resolution. See design/grammar-rationale.md for the specific
     design decisions that buy this property, and design/static-rules.md for the
     well-formedness rules that are deliberately NOT encoded here.

     It is LR(1) / LALR(1) everywhere but one place. `GenericExpr` -- type
     arguments in expression position, `f<A>(x)` -- shares its first token
     with the comparison operator, and which one a `<` is cannot be known
     until the `>` that would close the list is found. That is unbounded
     lookahead, and it is the reason the two implementations of this file both
     say so: `parser.rs` looks ahead for the `>` and backtracks, and the
     generated tree-sitter grammar declares the one `@conflicts` group below.

     What keeps the grammar unambiguous through it is that CmpExpr is
     NON-ASSOCIATIVE. `a < b > (c)` derives only as a call, because the
     comparison reading of it would be a chain, and a chain is not derivable
     from CmpExpr at all.

     The type system is NOMINAL throughout: every type has a declaration, and
     trait conformance is declared rather than inferred from shape.
   ========================================================================== *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   GENERATING A PARSER

   This file is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for Buri's syntax, and it is executable
   twice over. `editors/tree-sitter-buri/grammar.js` is GENERATED from it by
   `cli/src/documentation/grammar.rs`; a test regenerates it and fails on any
   difference, so the two cannot drift:

       BURI_BLESS=1 cargo test -p buri --test language corpus::the_tree_sitter_grammar

   And `cli/src/parsing/parser.rs` is the hand-written implementation, held to
   this file by the corpus: every source the compiler parses, the generated
   tree-sitter grammar must parse with no error node, and every source the
   compiler rejects *in the parser* it must reject
   (`editors/tree-sitter-buri/check.sh`).

   A context-free grammar does not say what a syntax tree should look like,
   and a generated parser needs to be told. Everything it needs beyond the
   productions is written as a directive — an ordinary EBNF comment whose
   first character is `@`, applying to the production that follows it. Nothing
   a production already states is stated again: a node's name comes from the
   production's name, an operator's precedence from its position in the
   cascade, and its associativity from the shape of its production.

     `@node NAME`   the node's name, where the derived one would be wrong
     `@hidden`      the node is inlined into its parent and never appears
     `@inline`      no rule at all; the body is substituted at each use
     `@external`    the terminal comes from the hand-written scanner
     `@token`       one lexical token; the pattern is compiled from the body
     `@regex RE`    the pattern, where the lexical grammar states it in prose
     `@prose`       the body is documentation, and is not read as grammar
     `@as NAME`     the syntax tree represents this as another production
     `@prec ...`    a disambiguation a generated parser needs and LR(1) does
                    not; the shape of the production is unaffected.
                    `left N`, `right N`, `N`, or `dynamic N` — the last being
                    which of two *completed* parses to keep, and the only one
                    that displaces the number the cascade would hand a
                    production, because a static number would mean the second
                    parse was never built
     `@raw JS`      the escape hatch: this rule, in the generator's own terms

   Five more describe the whole grammar rather than one production, and are
   written out below: `@grammar`, `@word`, `@extras`, `@externals` and
   `@words`. A sixth, `@cascade`, states the precedence ladder and lives with
   the expressions in section 5, where a reader is already looking at it. A
   seventh, `@conflicts`, declares a place a generated parser must explore two
   readings at once and keep whichever completes; this grammar has exactly
   one, at `GenericExpr`, and it is the same place the LR(1) claim above is
   qualified.

   A line that opens with an `@` is a directive; that is why the list of them
   above writes each one in backticks.

   @grammar buri

   @word IDENT
   @extras Whitespace LineComment DocComment ModuleDoc BlockComment

   The scanner keeps no stack: it is told which tokens are valid where it
   stands, and "a template span is valid here" is the whole of the state. The
   order below is the order of the enum in `src/scanner.c`.

   @externals STRING TEMPLATE_HEAD TEMPLATE_SPAN TEMPLATE_TAIL BlockComment

   How a production's name becomes a node's name: split on the capitals,
   expand each word, join with underscores. A plural is its singular's
   expansion with an `s`, so `Params` follows `Param` without being listed.

   @words Decl=declaration Expr=expression Fn=function Lit=literal
          Stmt=statement Param=parameter Spec=specifier Arg=argument
          Init=initializer

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. COMPILATION UNIT
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* @node source_file *)
Module          ::= Item*

(* @hidden *)
Item            ::= Import
                  | ReExport
                  | Exportable
                  | ImplDecl               (* never exported as a whole; a  *)
                  | DeriveDecl             (* method inside one may be       *)
                  | TestDecl               (* test sources only; SPEC 11.2   *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. IMPORTS

   The module path comes first so that an editor knows which module is being
   named before the specifier list is typed, and can complete it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

Import          ::= "from" path=STRING "import" ImportClause ";"

(* A namespace import MUST be named: `import * as list` is the only wildcard
   form, and bare `import *` is not derivable. Nothing ever enters a scope
   without a name written in the importing file.

   @hidden *)
ImportClause    ::= "{" ImportSpecs? "}"
                  | NamespaceImport

NamespaceImport ::= "*" "as" name=IDENT

ImportSpecs     ::= ImportSpec ("," ImportSpec)* ","?

ImportSpec      ::= name=IDENT ("as" alias=IDENT)?

(* A re-export publishes a name this module imported, and mirrors `import` in
   both shape and restriction: there is no `export *`, so every name a module
   publishes is written in that module's own source. A library's lib.buri is
   made of these. *)
ReExport        ::= "from" path=STRING "export" "{" ImportSpecs? "}" ";"

(* STRING in an Import or ReExport is a module path. It has two roots, and it
   names one of two kinds of thing:
     "core/list"                   a standard library module — a surface
     "//lib/money"                 a package's surface, its "lib.buri"
     "//lib/money/testing"         a package's testing surface
     "//lib/money/cents.buri"      a file, which only its own package may name
   A surface is what a package publishes, so naming it is naming the package,
   and that is the same string the dependents declare. Everything else is one
   file among many, so the path is the file's name, extension and all.
   There are no relative paths: a leading "." is not a module path. *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. DECLARATIONS
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

Exportable      ::= "export"? ExportableBody

(* @hidden *)
ExportableBody  ::= FnDecl
                  | StructDecl
                  | EnumDecl
                  | TypeAliasDecl
                  | LetDecl
                  | TraitDecl
                  | EffectDecl
                  | ContextDecl            (* exportable only from a test-only *)
                                           (* module; SPEC 11.3               *)

(* A declaration with no body ends in `;` instead of a Block. It is how the
   bundled standard library declares the operations the runtime supplies
   (`export fn len(self): Int;`, std/str.buri:18) and, as MethodSig below,
   how a trait or an effect states a method.

   The two are one production because they are one shape to the parser, which
   reads the `;` wherever a body could stand. Where it is *allowed* is a
   separate rule, and not a syntactic one: outside a trait, an effect, or a
   module of the bundled standard library, a body-less FnDecl parses and is
   then rejected, so that the diagnostic can say what is missing rather than
   what was unexpected. *)
FnDecl          ::= "fn" name=IDENT GenericParams? "(" Params? ")"
                    ":" return_type=Type (body=Block | ";")

(* A function is a METHOD if and only if it is declared inside an `impl` block
   and takes `self` first. Neither half is optional: a top-level `fn` may not
   take `self`, and a FnDecl inside an `impl` block must.
   A function is EFFECTFUL if and only if it has a `ctx` parameter, which comes
   first, or immediately after `self`. Both are declarations, not conventions
   about position or type. *)
Params          ::= SelfParam ("," CtxParam)? ("," Param)* ","?
                  | CtxParam ("," Param)* ","?
                  | Param ("," Param)* ","?

(* `self` is the one parameter that writes no type, because the declaration it
   sits in has already written it: the `impl` head for a method, and the
   implementing type — `Self` — for a MethodSig. The type checker reads it from
   there rather than from the signature, so the two can no longer disagree.
   `self: Type` was the older spelling and is now the `self-with-a-type` error,
   which carries the edit that deletes the annotation. *)
SelfParam       ::= "self"
CtxParam        ::= "ctx" ":" Type
Param           ::= name=IDENT ":" type=Type

GenericParams   ::= "<" GenericParam ("," GenericParam)* ","? ">"
GenericParam    ::= name=IDENT (":" Bounds)?
Bounds          ::= NamedType ("+" NamedType)*

(* Struct fields are module-private unless exported. That is the only
   visibility mechanism a type has -- record structs and tuple structs both use
   it, and there is no `opaque` modifier. An enum works the other way round:
   the enum is the unit, and its variants and their payload fields are exported
   exactly when it is. *)
StructDecl      ::= "struct" name=IDENT GenericParams? StructBody

(* @inline *)
StructBody      ::= FieldDeclsBlock                  (* record struct *)
                  | TupleFieldsBlock                 (* tuple struct  *)

FieldDeclsBlock ::= "{" FieldDecls? "}"
TupleFieldsBlock
                ::= "(" TupleFields? ")" ";"

FieldDecls      ::= FieldDecl ("," FieldDecl)* ","?
FieldDecl       ::= "export"? name=IDENT ":" type=Type

TupleFields     ::= TupleField ("," TupleField)* ","?
TupleField      ::= "export"? Type

(* A variant writes no `export`, because the enum has already written it: an
   exported enum exports every variant, and a private one exports none. The
   same goes for the fields of a record payload, which is why they are their
   own production rather than the FieldDecls a struct uses. Writing `export`
   before a variant was the older spelling and is now the `variant-export`
   error, which carries the edit that deletes it. *)
EnumDecl        ::= "enum" name=IDENT GenericParams? "{" Variants? "}"
Variants        ::= Variant ("," Variant)* ","?
Variant         ::= name=IDENT VariantPayload?

(* @hidden *)
VariantPayload  ::= "(" Types ")"
                  | "{" VariantFields "}"

VariantFields   ::= VariantField ("," VariantField)* ","?
VariantField    ::= name=IDENT ":" type=Type

TypeAliasDecl   ::= "type" name=IDENT GenericParams? "=" Type ";"

(* A module-level binding. There is one binding keyword in the language, so
   this is the same `let` a block uses; what differs is the position, and with
   it two requirements the position imposes. The name is an IDENT rather than a
   Pattern, because a module's surface is a list of names and a destructuring
   would hide them. The type is written rather than inferred, for the same
   reason `FnDecl` writes its return type: a module's surface is read far more
   often than it is compiled. *)
LetDecl         ::= "let" name=IDENT ":" type=Type "=" value=Expr ";"


(* --- Traits and effects ----------------------------------------------------
   A trait is an interface: a named set of method signatures. Conformance is
   NOMINAL -- a type satisfies a trait only where an `impl` or `derive` says
   so, never by accident of shape.

   An effect is the same declaration with one extra consequence: its
   implementors are effect-carrying, so they may only be passed as `self` or
   `ctx`. Effects may be declared only by platform modules, and no type may
   implement both an effect and a trait.

   The GenericParams the grammar admits here are refused statically
   [generic-effect-unsupported]: an `impl` names a trait without arguments, so
   there is nowhere to bind them. The parse is kept so that the diagnostic can
   point at them and say so. A *method's* own GenericParams, below, are
   supported and in use. *)

TraitDecl       ::= "trait" name=IDENT GenericParams? "{" MethodSig* "}"
EffectDecl      ::= "effect" name=IDENT GenericParams? "{" MethodSig* "}"

(* MethodSig is the body-less FnDecl, named separately because here it is the
   only form: a trait or an effect states signatures and supplies no bodies,
   so the `;` is required rather than merely admitted. A syntax tree does not
   distinguish them -- one node accepts both, and the highlight queries do not
   have to name two -- so the requirement is checked where the rest of the
   trait's rules are.

   @as FnDecl *)
MethodSig       ::= "fn" name=IDENT GenericParams? "(" Params? ")" ":" Type ";"

(* An `impl` with no `for` clause declares the type's own methods. It is the
   only place a method may be declared: every FnDecl inside one takes `self`
   first, and no FnDecl outside one may. With the `for` clause it declares
   conformance and supplies the trait's methods.

   Both positions are a full Type rather than a NamedType, because `[T]` has
   methods of its own. That the trait position must name a trait is a static
   rule, checked where the rest of trait resolution is. One token of lookahead
   after the first Type decides the form.

   The two forms differ in one more way, which is why they are written out
   separately here. A method of the type's own is part of the type's API and
   is exported on its own terms, so it may be written `export fn` — every
   `impl` in the standard library and in cli/tests/example does. A method that
   satisfies a trait may not: conformance is a property of the type and is
   visible wherever the type is, so there is nothing for an `export` to say
   and writing one is an error. *)
ImplDecl        ::= "impl" GenericParams? type=Type "{" ImplMethod* "}"
                  | "impl" GenericParams? type=Type "for" trait=Type
                    "{" FnDecl* "}"

ImplMethod      ::= "export"? FnDecl

DeriveDecl      ::= "derive" NamedType ("," NamedType)* "for" type=NamedType ";"


(* --- Contexts --------------------------------------------------------------
   A context binds each effect it provides to a value implementing it. It is
   the only construct in which more than one effect-carrying value may appear,
   and the only place a context is created rather than received (SPEC.md 11.3).

   Two forms, told apart by one token of lookahead after `context`: an IDENT
   opens a declaration, a `{` opens an expression. A declaration takes no
   parameters and is constructed by calling it -- `Fixture()` -- so each use
   gets a fresh one. Where either form may appear is a static rule, not a
   grammar one.

   The left side of a binding is a NamedType because it names an effect, which
   may be written qualified (`effects.Alloc`). The right side is any expression. *)

ContextDecl     ::= "context" name=IDENT ContextBody

ContextExpr     ::= "context" ContextBody

ContextBody     ::= "{" Spread? ContextBindings? "}"
ContextBindings ::= ContextBinding ("," ContextBinding)* ","?
ContextBinding  ::= effect=NamedType ":" value=Expr

(* Spread is the same production a struct literal uses (section 5): a context
   and a struct literal are both a brace-delimited list of `Name: value` pairs
   over an optional base. What differs is that the name on the left is an
   effect rather than a field, which is visible in its case. *)


(* --- Tests -----------------------------------------------------------------
   Legal only in a test source (SPEC.md 11.2). A test takes no parameters and
   returns nothing: it builds whatever context it needs with a ContextExpr, or
   calls one the file declared, and passes unless an assertion fails. The name
   is a STRING because test names are prose. *)

TestDecl        ::= "test" name=STRING body=Block


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. TYPES

   `<` and `>` are unambiguous inside Type because the type grammar has no
   comparison operators. In expressions they are not, and `GenericExpr` in
   section 5 is where that is settled.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* @hidden *)
Type            ::= FnType
                  | PrimaryType

FnType          ::= "fn" "(" Types? ")" "=>" Type

Types           ::= Type ("," Type)* ","?

(* @hidden *)
PrimaryType     ::= NamedType
                  | SelfType
                  | UnitType
                  | TupleType
                  | ArrayType
                  | GroupedType

(* `Self` is the implementing type; legal only inside a trait, effect, or
   impl body. *)
SelfType        ::= "Self"

(* The unit type and its only value are both written `()`. There is no empty
   record, because there are no records. *)
UnitType        ::= "(" ")"

NamedType       ::= TypePath TypeArgs?
TypePath        ::= IDENT ("." IDENT)*

TypeArgs        ::= "<" Type ("," Type)* ","? ">"

ArrayType       ::= "[" Type "]"

GroupedType     ::= "(" Type ")"

(* Tuples have arity >= 2. `(T)` is grouping and `()` is unit. *)
TupleType       ::= "(" Type ("," Type)+ ","? ")"


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. EXPRESSIONS

   Precedence, lowest to highest:

       lambda                (top level only; never a sub-operand)
       ||                    left
       ??                    right
       &&                    left
       == != < <= > >=       non-associative
       |                     left
       ^                     left
       &                     left
       + -                   left
       * / %                 left
       - ! ~                 prefix
       postfix chain         . .N (args) [i] ? <T> {fields}

   That table is the cascade below, and a generated parser is given it as a
   number per level rather than as eleven nested productions -- otherwise
   every literal in a program would sit under nine wrappers. The number is the
   level's position in the list; the associativity is the shape of the
   production, since `X ::= X op Y | Y` is left, `X ::= Y op X | Y` is right,
   and `X ::= Y op Y | Y` is neither. The nine binary levels are one node,
   because a highlight query should not have to know the whole chain.

   Non-associativity is the one thing a generated parser is not told: it reads
   `a < b < c` as left-associative and the compiler rejects the chain with its
   own message, which is better than an editor showing a syntax error where
   the compiler has a sentence.

   @cascade _operand
     OrExpr CoalesceExpr AndExpr CmpExpr BitOrExpr BitXorExpr
     BitAndExpr AddExpr MulExpr           = binary_expression
     UnaryExpr                            = unary_expression
     PostfixExpr

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* @hidden *)
Expr            ::= Lambda
                  | OrExpr

Lambda          ::= "fn" "(" LambdaParams? ")" (":" Type)? "=>" Expr
LambdaParams    ::= LambdaParam ("," LambdaParam)* ","?
LambdaParam     ::= name=IDENT (":" Type)?

OrExpr          ::= OrExpr "||" CoalesceExpr
                  | CoalesceExpr

CoalesceExpr    ::= AndExpr "??" CoalesceExpr
                  | AndExpr

AndExpr         ::= AndExpr "&&" CmpExpr
                  | CmpExpr

CmpExpr         ::= BitOrExpr CmpOp BitOrExpr
                  | BitOrExpr

(* @inline *)
CmpOp           ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="

BitOrExpr       ::= BitOrExpr "|" BitXorExpr
                  | BitXorExpr

BitXorExpr      ::= BitXorExpr "^" BitAndExpr
                  | BitAndExpr

BitAndExpr      ::= BitAndExpr "&" AddExpr
                  | AddExpr

AddExpr         ::= AddExpr ("+" | "-") MulExpr
                  | MulExpr

MulExpr         ::= MulExpr ("*" | "/" | "%") UnaryExpr
                  | UnaryExpr

UnaryExpr       ::= ("-" | "!" | "~") UnaryExpr
                  | PostfixExpr
                  | BlockLikeExpr

(* Brace-terminated, self-delimiting expressions. They may appear as operands
   of binary operators, but they may NOT be the head of a postfix chain --
   that restriction is what keeps `if (c) { a } else { b } { x: 1 }`
   from having two parses.

   @hidden *)
BlockLikeExpr   ::= Block
                  | AnonymousStructLit
                  | IfExpr
                  | MatchExpr
                  | ContextExpr

(* Method calls have no production of their own: `sq.area()` is FieldExpr
   followed by CallExpr, identical in shape to a field access or a module
   member access. Which one it is, is settled during name resolution.

   @hidden *)
PostfixExpr     ::= PrimaryExpr
                  | FieldExpr
                  | TupleIndexExpr
                  | CallExpr
                  | IndexExpr
                  | TryExpr
                  | GenericExpr
                  | StructLit

FieldExpr       ::= value=PostfixExpr "." field=IDENT        (* field / member / method *)
TupleIndexExpr  ::= value=PostfixExpr "." index=INT          (* tuple element access    *)
CallExpr        ::= function=PostfixExpr "(" Args? ")"       (* call                    *)
IndexExpr       ::= value=PostfixExpr "[" Expr "]"           (* index -> Option<T>      *)
TryExpr         ::= PostfixExpr "?"                          (* try-propagate           *)

(* Explicit type arguments: `list.empty<Int>()`, `identity<Int>`. The one
   production in this file that is not LR(1), and the one place a generated
   parser is told to explore two readings at once:

     @conflicts _operand GenericExpr

   The dynamic precedence is which reading to keep when both complete, and it
   is a *replacement* for the postfix level's number rather than an addition
   to it -- a static number would settle the conflict before the second
   reading was ever built, and `a < b` would stop being a comparison.

   Only `a < b > (c)` and `a<b>{ f: 1 }` reach the tie-break, and neither is a
   comparison in this grammar: CmpExpr does not chain, so the reading that
   loses is not a parse of anything.

   @prec dynamic 1 *)
GenericExpr     ::= PostfixExpr TypeArgs                     (* type arguments          *)
StructLit       ::= type=PostfixExpr StructLitBody           (* struct literal          *)

(* A `{` following a path is ALWAYS a struct literal; nothing competes, so
   field shorthand is unambiguous here. *)
StructLitBody   ::= "{" Spread? FieldInits? "}"
Spread          ::= ".." Expr ","?
FieldInits      ::= AnyFieldInit ("," AnyFieldInit)* ","?

(* Two productions rather than one with an optional value, because
   AnonymousStructLit below has to say "a keyed field, or a shorthand field
   that is not the only thing in the braces", and one production cannot be
   quantified that way. The split is worth having on its own: a shorthand field
   names a value in scope as well as a field.

   @hidden *)
AnyFieldInit    ::= FieldInit | FieldShorthand
FieldInit       ::= name=IDENT ":" value=Expr
FieldShorthand  ::= name=IDENT

(* A literal with no head at all. Its type is the one the expression is checked
   against, so it is settled by the checker (design/static-rules.md rule 1) and
   not here: no production below consults a type, and none needs to.

   A bare `{` otherwise opens a Block, and a Block is `{ Stmt* Expr? }`. So the
   rule is the two tokens after the `{`: a `..`, or a `name ":"`. Neither can
   begin a Stmt or an Expr, so nothing competes for them, and every other `{`
   is a block -- `{ }`, `{ name }` and `{ name "," ... }` included. Two tokens
   of lookahead decide, so the grammar stays LR(1) and this declares no
   conflict.

   The first field carries the whole decision, which is why FieldShorthand is
   not among the openers: admitting it would make `{ name }` and `{ name "," }`
   two different constructs a comma apart. Shorthand after the first field is
   free, because by then the `{` is settled: `{ hi: hi, hello }` is a literal. *)
AnonymousStructLit
                ::= "{" AnonymousFields "}"

(* @hidden *)
AnonymousFields ::= Spread FieldInits?
                  | FieldInit ("," FieldInits?)?

Args            ::= Expr ("," Expr)* ","?

(* `self` and `ctx` are keywords, so IDENT does not cover them, but both are
   ordinary values once bound and appear in expressions like any other name.

   @hidden *)
PrimaryExpr     ::= Literal
                  | IDENT
                  | SelfExpr
                  | CtxExpr
                  | VariantExpr
                  | UnitExpr
                  | ArrayExpr
                  | TupleExpr
                  | GroupedExpr

SelfExpr        ::= "self"                    (* in a method body        *)
CtxExpr         ::= "ctx"                     (* the context in scope    *)
VariantExpr     ::= "." name=IDENT            (* inferred-type variant   *)

UnitExpr        ::= "(" ")"

ArrayExpr       ::= "[" (Expr ("," Expr)* ","?)? "]"

(* Arity >= 2; `(e)` is grouping and `()` is unit. *)
TupleExpr       ::= "(" Expr ("," Expr)+ ","? ")"

GroupedExpr     ::= "(" Expr ")"

IfExpr          ::= "if" "(" condition=Expr ")" consequence=Block
                    "else" alternative=ElseBranch

(* @inline *)
ElseBranch      ::= Block
                  | IfExpr

MatchExpr       ::= "match" "(" value=Expr ")" "{" MatchArms? "}"
MatchArms       ::= MatchArm ("," MatchArm)* ","?
MatchArm        ::= pattern=Pattern Guard? "=>" value=Expr
Guard           ::= "if" Expr

(* There are no loops. Iteration is recursion (guaranteed tail-call
   eliminated) or a fold. See design/non-goals.md. *)


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. BLOCKS AND STATEMENTS

   A block is a sequence of `let` bindings followed by a result expression.
   There are no expression statements, no `return`, and no loops.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* The result expression is optional *syntactically*. A block without one has
   no value, which is an error everywhere a block may stand, but it is the
   same kind of error as a block whose value is of the wrong type -- so it is
   reported by the checker, which can say what the block was expected to
   produce, rather than by the parser, which cannot. *)
Block           ::= "{" Stmt* Expr? "}"

(* `let` is the only statement outside a test source. ExprStmt is legal only in
   a test source and only when its type is () -- both static rules, SPEC 11.2 --
   so nothing must-use can be dropped by it. It stays LR(1): after an Expr, a
   ";" means statement and a "}" means result expression.

   @hidden *)
Stmt            ::= LetStmt
                  | ExprStmt

(* The second form binds a context. It takes no pattern and no type annotation,
   because a context's type is generated and never written (SPEC.md 11.3), and
   it is legal only where a context may be built. After "let", one token of
   lookahead decides which form this is. *)
LetStmt         ::= "let" pattern=Pattern (":" Type)? "=" value=Expr ";"
                  | "let" "ctx" "=" value=Expr ";"

(* Any Expr, not only a call: what restricts it is the static rule on its type,
   not the grammar. The ";" is what tells a statement from the result
   expression, so every one of them carries it -- a "{"-initial expression
   included, which is what keeps a block's last item unambiguous. *)
ExprStmt        ::= Expr ";"


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. PATTERNS

   A bare IDENT is ALWAYS a binding. Enum variants must be written qualified
   (`Option.None`) or with the inferred-type dot form (`.None`). This is what
   makes patterns parseable without name resolution.

   The `@prec` directives below are the one place a generated parser is told
   something LR(1) settles for itself. An IDENT may open a BindPattern or a
   PathPattern, and one token of lookahead decides which; a parser that
   explores both needs to be told which to keep.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* @hidden *)
Pattern         ::= OrPattern
                  | PatternNoOr

(* @prec left *)
OrPattern       ::= PatternNoOr ("|" PatternNoOr)+

(* @hidden *)
PatternNoOr     ::= BindPattern
                  | WildcardPattern
                  | LiteralPattern
                  | PathPattern
                  | UnitPattern
                  | TuplePattern
                  | ArrayPattern
                  | GroupedPattern

(* @prec 1 *)
BindPattern     ::= name=IDENT ("@" PatternNoOr)?

WildcardPattern ::= "_"

LiteralPattern  ::= "-"? INT
                  | "-"? FLOAT
                  | STRING
                  | CHAR
                  | True
                  | False

(* @prec 2 *)
PathPattern     ::= IDENT PatternPayload                  (* struct / tuple-struct *)
                  | QualifiedPath PatternPayload?         (* Enum.Variant          *)
                  | "." name=IDENT PatternPayload?        (* .Variant              *)

QualifiedPath   ::= IDENT "." IDENT ("." IDENT)*

PatternPayload  ::= "(" Patterns? ")"
                  | "{" FieldPatterns? "}"

Patterns        ::= Pattern ("," Pattern)* ","?

FieldPatterns   ::= FieldPattern ("," FieldPattern)* ("," Rest)? ","?
                  | Rest ","?
FieldPattern    ::= name=IDENT (":" Pattern)?

(* The `..` that says a struct pattern names only some of the fields. *)
Rest            ::= ".."

UnitPattern     ::= "(" ")"

TuplePattern    ::= "(" Pattern ("," Pattern)+ ","? ")"

GroupedPattern  ::= "(" Pattern ")"

ArrayPattern    ::= "[" ArrayPatternBody? "]"

(* @hidden *)
ArrayPatternBody
                ::= Pattern ("," Pattern)* ("," RestPattern)? ","?
                  | RestPattern ","?
RestPattern     ::= ".." name=IDENT?


(* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. LITERALS
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* @hidden *)
Literal         ::= INT
                  | FLOAT
                  | STRING
                  | CHAR
                  | True
                  | False
                  | TemplateLiteral

True            ::= "true"
False           ::= "false"

(* String interpolation. The three token classes are produced by the lexer's
   template mode; the nesting is handled lexically so that the *syntactic*
   grammar stays context-free.

       "score: ${a + b} pts"
        \_TEMPLATE_HEAD_/     \_TEMPLATE_TAIL_/                          *)
TemplateLiteral ::= TEMPLATE_HEAD Expr (TEMPLATE_SPAN Expr)* TEMPLATE_TAIL


(* ==========================================================================
   LEXICAL GRAMMAR
   ========================================================================== *)

(* Source text is UTF-8. Whitespace and comments are insignificant and may
   appear between any two tokens. Buri is NOT newline-sensitive.

   @token @inline *)
Whitespace      ::= (" " | "\t" | "\r" | "\n")+

Comment         ::= LineComment | BlockComment

(* A `///` is a doc comment and a `////` deliberately is not, so the longer run
   of slashes is an ordinary comment. The lexer settles that by longest match;
   a generated one has to be told, which is what these three patterns are.

   @regex \/\/(?:[^!\/\n][^\n]*|\/\/+[^\n]*|) *)
LineComment     ::= "//" (any character except "\n")*

(* @external @prose *)
BlockComment    ::= "/*" (BlockComment | any character)* "*/"     (* nestable *)

(* @regex \/\/\/(?:[^\/\n][^\n]*|\/|) *)
DocComment      ::= "///" (any character except "\n")*            (* a LineComment
                                                                     attached to the
                                                                     next Item      *)

(* @regex \/\/![^\n]* *)
ModuleDoc       ::= "//!" (any character except "\n")*            (* attached to the
                                                                     file itself    *)

(* A keyword is not a separate token class: it is an IDENT the lexer refuses to
   hand over, so the two lists below are subtracted from this one.

   @node identifier
   @regex [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* *)
IDENT           ::= (Letter | "_") (Letter | Digit | "_")*
                    minus Keyword minus ReservedWord

Letter          ::= "A".."Z" | "a".."z"
Digit           ::= "0".."9"

Keyword         ::= "as"     | "const"  | "context"| "ctx"    | "derive"
                  | "effect" | "else"   | "enum"   | "export" | "false"
                  | "fn"     | "for"    | "from"   | "if"     | "impl"
                  | "import" | "let"    | "match"  | "self"   | "Self"
                  | "struct" | "test"   | "trait"  | "true"   | "type"

(* `as` appears only in import specifiers. `for` only in `impl ... for ...`
   and `derive ... for ...`. There is no cast operator: numeric conversions
   are ordinary methods (SPEC.md 6.2.1).

   `const` is the one keyword no production names. It was how a module-level
   binding was spelled before there was one binding keyword, and it stays a
   keyword rather than becoming an ordinary word so that the source that still
   has it is answered with `const-declaration`, which names `let` and carries
   the edit, instead of being read as a name and failing somewhere further on.

   `test` and `context` are reserved in every module, so no function is named
   either, but a TestDecl is legal only in a test source and a ContextDecl or
   ContextExpr only where SPEC.md 11.3 says -- both static rules, not grammar
   ones. `assert` is NOT a keyword: assertions are the ordinary module
   `core/testing/assert`, and the name comes from `import * as assert`.

   `ctx` is a keyword, so it is legal as a parameter name in the fixed position
   CtxParam gives it, and as a LetStmt binding where a context may be built.
   Both restrictions are static rules. *)

(* Reserved but unused in v0.3; rejected by the lexer so that later versions
   can claim them without breaking source compatibility. A generated parser is
   NOT told about them: it would then refuse the program the compiler's own
   error message is about, and the language server is where that message
   belongs. *)
ReservedWord    ::= "async"  | "await"  | "break"  | "continue" | "do"
                  | "in"     | "is"     | "loop"   | "module" | "mut"
                  | "opaque" | "panic"  | "pub"    | "return" | "unreachable"
                  | "use"    | "when"   | "where"  | "while"  | "with"
                  | "yield"

(* --- Numbers ---------------------------------------------------------------
   A FLOAT must begin with a digit: `.5` is not a literal. This is what makes
   tuple access `pair.0` lex as IDENT "." INT rather than IDENT FLOAT.        *)

(* @node integer_literal @token *)
INT             ::= DecInt | HexInt | OctInt | BinInt
DecInt          ::= Digit (Digit | "_")*
HexInt          ::= "0x" HexDigit (HexDigit | "_")*
OctInt          ::= "0o" OctDigit (OctDigit | "_")*
BinInt          ::= "0b" BinDigit (BinDigit | "_")*
HexDigit        ::= Digit | "a".."f" | "A".."F"
OctDigit        ::= "0".."7"
BinDigit        ::= "0" | "1"

(* @node float_literal @token *)
FLOAT           ::= DecInt "." DecInt Exponent?
                  | DecInt Exponent
Exponent        ::= ("e" | "E") ("+" | "-")? DecInt

(* --- Text ---------------------------------------------------------------- *)

(* A string is scanned by hand rather than matched, because a `${` opens a hole
   and the `}` that closes it is not the `}` that closes a block.

   @node string_literal @external *)
STRING          ::= '"' StrChar* '"'

(* @node char_literal @token *)
CHAR            ::= "'" (CharChar | Escape) "'"

(* A prose body: quoting the characters it is about would need the quotes it
   is about. It is documentation, and nothing reads it as grammar.

   @prose *)
StrChar         ::= any character other than a double quote, a backslash,
                    or an unescaped dollar-brace
                  | Escape

(* @regex [^'\\] *)
CharChar        ::= any character other than a single quote or a backslash

Escape          ::= "\\" ("n" | "r" | "t" | "0" | "\\" | '"' | "'" | "$"
                          | "u" "{" HexDigit+ "}")

(* @node template_head @external *)
TEMPLATE_HEAD   ::= '"' StrChar* "${"

(* @node template_span @external *)
TEMPLATE_SPAN   ::= "}"  StrChar* "${"

(* @node template_tail @external *)
TEMPLATE_TAIL   ::= "}"  StrChar* '"'

(* The lexer keeps a stack of open interpolations so that a `}` closing a
   block inside a hole is distinguished from the `}` that resumes template
   text. This is the only mode-dependent part of the lexer. *)

(* --- Punctuation and operators ---------------------------------------------
   Longest-match wins. Note the deliberate absences:
     * there is no ">>" or "<<" token, so `Map<Str, List<Int>>` never
       mis-lexes; use bits.shl / bits.shr instead.
     * there is no "?." token, so `x?.field` is `x` `?` `.` `field`.
     * there is no "..." token; "``..``" serves in patterns and struct
       spreads alike.                                                        *)

Punctuation     ::= "{" | "}" | "(" | ")" | "[" | "]"
                  | "," | ";" | ":" | "::" | "." | ".." | "@" | "_"
                  | "=" | "=>"
                  | "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
                  | "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%"
                  | "&&" | "||" | "!" | "??"
                  | "&" | "|" | "^" | "~"
                  | "?"

(* Known lexical wart (shared with Rust): `t.0.1` lexes as `t` `.` `0.1`.
   Write `(t.0).1`. *)
(* Known lexical wart: `Foo<Bar<Int>>= x` lexes `>` `>=`. Write a space, i.e.
   `Foo<Bar<Int>> = x`. *)
